Date: Thu, 9 Dec 93 06:57:36 -0800
From: Romana Machado <romana@apple.com>
Subject: Memetic or Genetic Survival?

Eat me
Drink me

Nick Szabo writes:
>By definition people have their own interests and philosophies.
>The trick is to mold our children's motivations fit well with our
>future interests.  That's the whole reason to have children in the
>first place, the biological motivation having been rendered "impotent and
>obsolete" by birth control.   99% of families give in to the
>cultural pressure to let their kids fly in the memetic winds, in which
>cases I agree with the though others have expressed; it
>wasn't in their best interests to have children in the
>first place.  But with some forethought, a good channel blocker, home
>schooling, and the like it need not be that way.  Ask the Hutterites --
>world champions both in fertility and memetic isolation, by no means
>coincidentally.

I believe that molding other's motivations is best accomplished 
by memetic, not genetic, propagation. I don't believe that your
genes determine your meme-set, and you can find your memes
anywhere. Raising your children Catholic, or Hutterite, is
no guarantee that they will stay that way, or have any
respect for your beliefs once they encounter the 
meme market of the real social world.

Not only is the biological motivation dead,
the memetic motivation is useless. Perhaps a better path to
this goal would be to develop a concerted system of education
and competent memetic propagation. We are not going to get anywhere 
by blocking the flow of information. This seems horribly
anti-Extropian. 

Are these really "rational benefits"?

>* Extend one's web of trust, a la the British and Chinese clans
>cited by Duncan Frissel in his excellent article.  I've learned
>just recently yet again (a lesson I should have already known),
>that it's quite unwise to trust the judgement of somebody based
>on just mutual memetic interest. The emotional bonds of a
>good family are needed for the strongest kinds of trust, along
>with memetic agreement.  If either the long-standing emotional
>ties or the memetic ties are missing, that strong trust breaks
>down. 

I do not believe that the emotional bonds of a family and trust
are necessarily related. A good basis for trust is long-standing
relationship with repeated demonstrations of trustworthiness.
I have seen a _lot_ of cases where individuals behave in
 untrustworthy manner to their own families.
There are bad apples everywhere; be careful.

>* A special but important case of the web of trust, creating a
>network of people emotionally motivated to repair your cryonically
>frozen (carc)ass.  When you're in deep freeze, your fate completely
>in someone else's hands, blood is thicker than perpetuity.   (*)

Make yourself a valuable person. There are a lot of ways to 
do this (as Mr. Kreiger recently noted in the Jupiter brain
thread) that have nothing to do with blood relation. 
Your blood relations may suffer from a memetic infection
that makes them completely incapable of
seeing what's so special about you; your friends and
your memetic/intellectual descendants will have a 
much better understanding of your value.

>* Idealistic reasons
>-- the world is a better place with more smart, well educated people in it.
>-- the world is a better place with more Extropians.  In terms of future
>memetic impact, cultures like Islam with their explosively expansionary
>demographics will loom large in our futures.  Last year on sci.cryonics
>a cryonicist freind of mine posted his dream of being reanimated into
>a world without violence, homophobia, and other current ills.  But if
>Extropians get frozen without issue, the demographics
>favor the opposite outcome:  you are much more likely to be
>thawed out by a Mormon or Muslim than by an Extropian.

Educate, don't spawn. There are already billions & billions of humans, in which t
o propagate memes that block religious receptors,
that create happiness, long life, intentional community,
and  value for unique individuality.
Send your memes out into the memetic rough & tumble,
and see if they will fly!
It is possible to have many more memetic than genetic descendants.

>Extropianism is great for learning truths and the possible shapes
>of our future, but not so good at motivating effective action in the
>present that might have the greatest impact on that future

I disagree. "We are here and now among you." 
I am actively at work building an Extropian culture.
You perceive that nothing is happening? 
I have seen at first hand that Extropianism
affects many of the present actions of Extropians.
We all may not be aware of what's going on with others. We
must each determine the most effective action, and we
are not all going to agree, or necessarily work together.

Virtual communities,  new intentional families, mass media,
the free market of ideas, freedom of association/dissociation
and other current developments in social culture make conditions
for memetic propagation almost ideal. 


