Would a non-territorial species necessarily be non-expanding?

I've thought about this in regards to matter hackers--I've read a
piece by a matter hacker about his weekend at an sf con, and how
he revised and explored the hotel. (He had an assumption that
he could repair any damage he might do, and leave things in
better shape as he repaired them.)

The libertarians here are probably horrified at the idea of this
guy, but I can see him answering that letting things go downhill
just because they belong to someone else has drawbacks, too.

Anyway, I've speculated about what a species where his attitude
is the default would be like, and I've gotten as far as deciding
that the young would be socialized into leaving documentation
instead of respecting property. Any other ideas?

Nancy Lebovitz

P.S. I've read another piece by him about going to a Rainbow Gathering--
he learns that being around incompetent greedy people with no sense
of property isn't fun. (Everyone at the Gathering wasn't like that--
I think that he only ran into one Bad Example--but it was enough to
notice a problem with the No Property concept.)

