rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray) says:

>Charlie Stross writes:
>> A royal commission in the UK recently intensely annoyed the government
>> (which is strong on law'n'order, it likes to think) by reporting that
>> there is no correlation between broken/one-parent families and crime:
>> the correlation is between relative poverty and crime.
        :
>  Such studies are rarely scientific and full of holes. Does this study
>also explain the rise in _violent_ crime. In the 50s and 60s, when someone
>robbed a grocery store at gunpoint, they would take the money and leave.  
>Now, it's common for the gunmen to visciously gun down the clerk and innocent
>bystanders. 

Er, Ray, read what I wrote: *** "in the UK" *** !

>  I don't think the relative gap between rich/poor explains it at all. Hell,
>in the US there is a phenomena of _middle class_ crime and even rich kid
>crime.

What they found was a direct, proportional increase in crime that
matched the differential between rich and poor; and this varied from
neighbourhood to neighbourhood. The gap between richest and poorest
ten percentile has been widening in the UK (note: this may be invalid
in your neck of the world) for the past decade, and the crime rate has
risen in proportion. Note that crimes of violence have topped out and,
across the board, are stable or declining; but crimes against property
(e.g. car theft) are still rising.

> My own opinion, _illegal physically addictive drugs_ When you see someone
>else with a lifestyle you want, you are envious, but your inhibitions and
>human empathy usually prevent you from killing them or even stealing from
>them. I grew up with a family income <$25k/yr so I know what it's like.
>However, if you are physically addicted to a drug which you can't afford,
>therein lies the problem. I claim that addicts will resort to far more
>desparate measures to satisfy their craving than a poverty stricken
>individual.

Well, this is tautological. It's also true to say that the cost of
pharmaceutical grade heroin is about $10/20 grams. I know; I used to
stock the stuff (legally!) when I was a pharmacist. *IF* the stuff was
legal (and subject to controls no tighter than those currently imposed
on liquor) the average addict could support a habit even on welfare.

[ Really tragic story elided. You have my sympathy.]

>  No, I don't buy this liberal theory of crime at all. It doesn't explain
>squat and is a mighty _conveinient_ excuse for raising taxes and
>increasing socialism. The "War on Poverty" in the US only succeeded
>in letting crime and violence expand even more. It's a _cultural_
>problem -- the conservatives are right. It's a problem with people's
>ethics and values.

Cultural problem? Sure. Ethics and values? Sure. People with a sense
of purpose or self-discipline seem less likely to commit crimes -- or
at least, less likely to do so casually. And sure, a causal link
between crime and income differential is a convenient excuse for
raising taxes. But that's not the only way to respond to such a link,
and to ignore it is blind idiocy. A _sensible_ way to respond to it
would be not to build more expensive prisons, or to tax hard-working
people, but to (a) legalize a big chunk of the economy (putting
gangsters out of business as drug dealing suddenly becomes legal), 
(b) put the money spent on trying to prevent drug abuse into the public
education system (or back into the taxpayer's pockets -- YMMV), and 
(c) try to figure out how to give people a moral framework. Moral
absolutism or moral relativism, it doesn't matter. There's no
functional difference between "that's right and that's wrong" or "do
unto others as you would be done by" as laws to live by -- both of
them offer a framework that discourages crime.

>  Anyway, I don't buy this "inequality of wealth" bs as an explanation of
>violent crime in America.

Understood. I wasn't asserting that it was an explanation of violent
crime in America! I was asserting that there was a probable causal
link between "inequality of wealth" and crime -- mostly _property_ 
crime -- in the UK. 



