Subject: Brave New Extropia? No Thanks.

>With regard to the "don't concede the argument" thread, I am reminded of 
my
>father's comment that you can never persuade someone in an argument, you
>can only provide him the arguments that he later uses to persuade 
>himself."

I had this experience with my work just recently.  Planning ahead for a
big confrontation, I concluded that no matter how well I proved my points,
the other side could not admit defeat immediately.  They would promise
that they could deliver, and would get together *after* the meeting was
over to find out if their promises were valid.

I warned my manaagement that this would occur and told them not to
judge the outcome of the meeting that day.  I asked them to have a
follow-up meeting with the other company's management a week later
and to *then* determine the results.

I was right!  It greatly added to my confidence and my management approval 
to realize that the other side could not admit defeat during the meeting.
I was merely providing them data which would be taken back to their lab and 
analyzed later.
