I partially agree with Charlie's concerns, but I think the right way, from
a PPL perspective, to phrase the quote is:

	Prisons should cost the subscribers relatively little.  The prisoners
	should turn a profit.

What I mean by this is that in a PPL, prisons may  have some value to the
PPL subscribers, so they should be willing to pay some amount for them, but
the need is likely to be much less than in current society and many of the
costs can be offset by requiring the prisoners to pay for most of their
expenses.  Indeed, any prisoner who can produce more than the total cost of
incaraceration should be able to keep all or most of the profit.  This
would reduce the incentive to turn the prison into a slave labor camp and
would provide the prisoner with savings to help in rejoining society upon
release.

