>Amara Graps aka graps@gal.arc.nasa.gov says:
> I'm back still trying to visualize tesseracts!

Here's a very simple system that I use for viewing tesseracts.
It involves looking at objects from the end that can fit into a lower
dimension, down its length out of the dimension.

Imagine a 0-dimensional universe as a dot.  A 0-dimensional being
would wonder what a 1-dimensional line looks like.  Imagine a dot
in your 0-dimensional universe.  Imagine a smaller or farther-away
dot within the first dot.  (I know real points can't get smaller, 
but just ignore my inconsistencies :-) )  Connect these two dots, 
and you have a 1-dimensional line with you looking down its length
through one end.

	@ tilted is / flattened out is ----

Imagine a 1-dimensional universe as a line.  A 1-dimensional being
would wonder what a 2-dimensional square looks like.  Imagine a line
in your 1-dimensional universe.  Imagine a smaller or farther-away
line within your first line.  Connect the ends of these two lines,
and you have a 2-dimensional square with you looking down its length
through one edge.
                                      _______
	|           |\                   |       |
	| tilted is | | flattened out is |       |
	|           | |                  |       |
	|           |/                   |_______|

Imagine a 2-dimensional universe as a plane.  A 2-dimensional being
would wonder what a 3-dimensional cube looks like.  Imagine a square
in your 2-dimensional universe.  Imagine a smaller or farther-away
square within your first square.  Connect the corners of these two squares,
and you have a 3-dimensional cube with you looking down its length
through one plane.

	                      ______
	 ______             /|____/ |
	|\ __ /| tilted is | |    | | flattened out is [insert 3D cube]
	| |  | |           | |    | |
	| |__| |           | |______|
	|/____\|           |/_____|/

Imagine a 3-dimensional universe as a plane.  A 3-dimensional being
would wonder what a 4-dimensional hypercube looks like.  Imagine a cube
in your 3-dimensional universe.  Imagine a smaller or farther-away
cube within your first cube.  Connect the corners of these two cubes,
and you have a 4-dimensional hypercube with you looking down its length
through one cube.
       ___________
      /         /| [This is supposed to be a cube within a cube]
	 /_\ ______/ |
	|\ /|____/ | |
	| | |    | | | tilted is [insert 3D hypercube]
	| | |    | | | 
	| | |______|_| flattened out is [insert 4D hypercube]
	| |/_____|/ /
	|//_______\/

Did this make sense, or are the Jupiter-sized brains sadly shaking
their planet-sized brains at the pity of Homo Obsoletus?
